  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 AM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAn area of showers is underway spanning from central IA through \nextreme western Indiana. Mesoanalysis shows warm air advection aloft \nalong with subtle near-surface convergence, and even modest elevated \ninstability (through central IL, fading well before the IN line). \nThis WAA and surface convergence lift north-northeast through early \nthis afternoon. Despite an 8,000 foot dry layer and a 10-degree dew \npoint depression, light rain is underway at Monticello, IN. Trends \nlead me to believe these showers will persist through at least the \nmorning hours. The centroid of high pressure is analyzed over the \ngreater Lansing area, suggesting the far northeast reaches of these \nshowers will be limited, battling additional dry air and subsidence. \nWill carry on with the continued Halloween message of isoalted \nshowers, especially in the morning. It does appear showers will\nmove out in time for trick-or- treat hours. Cloudy and somewhat\ncool with highs just beyond 60 degrees. \n\nMurky, spotty, and weakly forced shower chances linger through at \nleast Monday as high pressure gradually gives way to ridging ahead \nof deepening low over the Central US. This results in periods of low-\nconfidence 20-30% POPs. A weak shortwave moving through the OH River \nValley presents a 20% chance of showers for far southern reaches of \nthe forecast area Sunday afternoon, with perhaps a better moisture \nstream arriving overnight.\n\nThe primary item of interest in the extended forecast is the mid- \nlatitude cyclone moving through midweek. Very warm on Tuesday (near-\nrecord highs) ahead of this low as the jet stream becomes highly \namplified over the Midwest. Breezy with southerly wind gusts of 20-\n25 mph. The occluding low over far northern lower Michigan looks to \ndelay our best rain chance until late Tuesday night and Wednesday. \nHow cyclogenesis evolves over the mid-Mississippi thereafter \ndictates how much our rain will see, or whether the cold/occluded \nfrontal passage is our only chance for rain. Behind the low, still \nmild for early November with highs in the 60s and lows in the\n40s.",
